{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`.rebar/enrich-drain.lock` is an `O_EXCL` existence lock with no owner stamp and no\nstaleness path. `enrich_drain._acquire_advisory_lock` creates it and never writes to the\nfd; `_release_advisory_lock` unlinks it. Any drainer that dies between those two calls\nleaks a 0-byte file PERMANENTLY: every later drain sees `FileExistsError`, returns\n`None`, and `drain()` logs at INFO and returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}`. Enrichment\nthen stops happening with no error and no visible signal. The new `rebar doctor`\nlock-health report (task 513e-35e1-2c80-4428) found exactly this on this host's live\ntracker: a 0-byte drain lock held ~26 days with no drain process alive, so overlap\nenrichment has been silently skipped on that checkout for roughly a month.\n\n## Approach\nGive the drain lock the ownership discipline the store's mkdir write lock already has,\nby REUSING that machinery rather than writing a third locking dialect.\n\n1. **Stamp on acquire.** After the `O_EXCL` create succeeds, write\n   `lock_owner._owner_stamp()` (the existing v2 `host=/ns=/pid=/start=` line) into the\n   file. Best-effort, exactly like `lock.py`'s mkdir stamp write: a failed stamp leaves\n   an unstamped lock, which the wall-clock ceiling still bounds.\n2. **Adjudicate on collision.** Extract the decision table already inside\n   `lock_owner._mkdir_lock_is_stale` into a shared core\n\n       _stamp_is_stale(stamp, artifact_path, *, fcntl_held, unrecognised_via_ceiling)\n\n   taking an already-read stamp plus the path whose mtime measures the hold age.\n   `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` becomes a thin reader that delegates, and a new sibling\n   `stamped_file_is_stale(path)` reads the stamp from the FILE and delegates to the same\n   core with `fcntl_held=False`. No new threshold, no new pid probe, no second\n   heuristic: the pid-recycle qualification, the refuse-without-proof branches and the 1h\n   `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` backstop are inherited as-is.\n\n   `unrecognised_via_ceiling` is the ONLY behavioural knob, and it selects what happens to\n   a stamp that is not v2 (empty, or any other unrecognised text). Nothing else in the\n   core branches on it; `fcntl_held` does not and cannot select it.\n\n   - `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` passes `unrecognised_via_ceiling=False`, preserving TODAY's\n     routing exactly: a non-v2 stamp goes to `_legacy_stamp_is_stale`. Worked example: a\n     mkdir owner file containing `\"\"` parses as non-v2, reaches\n     `_legacy_stamp_is_stale(\"\")`, finds no `:`, and returns False — the same answer the\n     current code gives, so the mkdir leg is unchanged.\n   - `stamped_file_is_stale` passes `unrecognised_via_ceiling=True`: a non-v2 stamp is\n     treated as NO PROOF OF OWNERSHIP and falls to the ceiling. Worked example: today's\n     leaked 0-byte `enrich-drain.lock`, 26 days old, parses as non-v2, takes the ceiling\n     branch, is older than 3600s, and returns True — reclaimable. The same file 5 seconds\n     old (a drainer inside the create/stamp window) is under the ceiling and returns\n     False — respected.\n\n   The asymmetry is deliberate and load-bearing: the mkdir lock has a real legacy\n   `<host>:<pid>` dialect that must keep being honoured, while the drain lock never had\n   one, so for it \"unrecognised\" can only mean \"unstamped orphan\", which is precisely the\n   pre-fix shape this bug must make reclaimable.\n3. **Bounded, loud reclaim.** On `FileExistsError`, ask `stamped_file_is_stale`. If it\n   refuses, skip as today. If it says stale, log at WARNING naming the holder stamp and\n   the age (mirroring `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`'s disclosure), unlink, and retry the create\n   EXACTLY ONCE. A second `FileExistsError` means another drainer won the race, so we\n   skip — no loop, no unbounded retry. A lock held by a live, corroborated holder is\n   never broken, because the shared core refuses it.\n4. **Make the skip attributable.** `drain()`'s skip log moves from INFO to WARNING and\n   names the holder decoded from the stamp, so a wedge is visible without running doctor.\n\nWhy this shape: the drain lock is advisory only (it stops two drain PROCESSES from\nredundant work; the queue claim is separately optimistic), so a lost reclaim race costs\nat worst one redundant drain, never correctness. A single bounded retry therefore needs\nno cross-process arbitration.\n\n**Declared prerequisite.** `rebar doctor`'s drain leg currently reports\n`not-assessable`. That row lives in `_commands/doctor_locks.py`, a NEW module created by\ntask 513e-35e1-2c80-4428 (Gerrit change 1701, `LLM-Review +1`, awaiting `Verified`), so\nthe file does not exist in today's tree. This ticket declares `depends_on\n513e-35e1-2c80-4428` and is COMMITTED to landing as that change's stacked child: its\ncommit is authored on top of 1701's commit, so `doctor_locks.py` is a guaranteed edit\ntarget at implementation time and 1701 submits first. If 1701 merges before this change\nis pushed, the stack collapses to a plain rebase onto `origin/main`.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` — extract `_stamp_is_stale` core; add\n  `stamped_file_is_stale`; `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` delegates (behaviour preserved).\n- `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` — stamp on acquire; stale-reclaim with one bounded\n  retry; holder description helper; WARNING skip log.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py` — the drain leg decodes the stamp and reports a\n  real staleness verdict instead of `not-assessable`.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/doctor.schema.json`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/doctor.txt`,\n  `docs/cli-reference.md` — only if the drain row's documented vocabulary changes.\n- `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py` — the drain-lock tests below. The existing lock-owner\n  suites (`tests/unit/test_lock_age_ceiling.py`, `test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`,\n  `test_lock_container_recreate.py`) are the unchanged-behaviour regression guard on the\n  extraction and are expected to need no edit.\n- `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py` — drain-leg verdict tests.\n\n## Testing\n- **Dead holder is reclaimed** (the bug): write a drain lock file stamped for a\n  provably-dead pid on this host, then call `drain()`/`_acquire_advisory_lock` and\n  assert it acquires, that the lock file now carries OUR stamp, and that the reclaim\n  logged at WARNING.\n- **Legacy unstamped orphan is reclaimed**: a 0-byte drain lock back-dated past the\n  ceiling is acquired; a 0-byte lock created just now is NOT (the create/stamp window\n  is respected).\n- **Live holder is honoured**: a lock stamped with THIS live process is not broken —\n  `drain()` still returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}` and the file is byte-identical\n  afterwards. The existing `test_lock_held_skip` covers this shape and must keep passing.\n- **Foreign host / other namespace**: a stamp naming another host is refused while\n  young and reclaimed only past the ceiling — asserting the inherited\n  refuse-without-proof rule rather than a new one.\n- **Release still works**: acquire then release removes the file, so the happy path is\n  unchanged.\n- **`_mkdir_lock_is_stale` unchanged**: the existing lock-owner suite passes untouched,\n  which is the regression guard on the extraction.\n- **Doctor**: a drain lock stamped for a dead pid yields a `stale-lock` finding and exit\n  1; one stamped for a live pid yields no finding and exit 0; doctor still mutates\n  nothing (the read-only test still passes).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A dead holder no longer wedges enrichment: a test leaving a drain lock stamped for\n      a provably-dead pid asserts the next `drain()` returns a non-skipped result.\n- [ ] A pre-fix unstamped orphan is reclaimed: a test back-dating a 0-byte drain lock\n      past the ceiling asserts acquisition succeeds.\n- [ ] The create/stamp window is respected: a test with a 0-byte drain lock created just\n      now asserts acquisition is refused.\n- [ ] A live holder is still honoured: a test with a lock stamped for a live pid asserts\n      `drain()` returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}`.\n- [ ] That live holder's lock file is left byte-identical after the refused attempt,\n      asserted by comparing its bytes before and after.\n- [ ] An acquired drain lock carries the existing v2 stamp: a test asserts the file's\n      contents parse with `lock_owner._parse_v2_stamp` into our own host and pid.\n- [ ] No second staleness heuristic is introduced: a grep of the diff shows no new age\n      constant and no `os.kill`/pid probe deciding staleness outside `lock_owner`.\n- [ ] The reclaim is bounded: a test in which every create attempt raises\n      `FileExistsError` asserts `os.open` was called exactly twice.\n- [ ] The reclaim is loud: `caplog` shows a WARNING naming the reclaimed holder stamp and\n      the hold age.\n- [ ] A skipped drain is attributable: `caplog` shows a WARNING naming the holder.\n- [ ] `rebar doctor`'s drain leg reports a real staleness verdict rather than\n      `not-assessable`, asserted on the `--output json` row.\n- [ ] A dead-holder drain lock becomes a `stale-lock` finding and `rebar doctor` exits 1,\n      asserted in `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py`.\n- [ ] Doctor still reclaims nothing: the existing read-only test passes with the drain\n      lock file unchanged after a run.\n- [ ] All touched `src/rebar` files stay under the 800-LOC module cap, verified by the\n      module-size gate.\n- [ ] The full local gate set is green: the `check_*` gates, generators re-run with no\n      diff, `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and CI's pytest invocation."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786597147572993001,"uuid":"4d1e20a2-eeb0-4e94-bf87-2c6718baa33c"}